  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 127 PM CST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nShort Term (Today through Tuesday): \n\nAfternoon water vapor imagery depicts a broad upper-level ridge\ndominating the central CONUS, while a deepening shortwave trough\npushes ashore from the eastern Pacific. Beneath the subsidence\nof surface high pressure and mid-level ridging, central Illinois\nwill experience quiet, dry weather through Tuesday. Clear skies\nand light winds will promote a healthy diurnal temperature\ncurve, featuring afternoon highs reaching into the 50s and\novernight lows dipping into the upper 20s to around 30F.\n\n\nMid-Week Warming Trend (Wednesday through Friday): \n \nBy Wednesday, the eastern Pacific shortwave will lift \nnortheastward across the Intermountain West, nudging the upper \nridge axis and surface high pressure center into the eastern \nCONUS. As this shortwave tracks well to our north and east, it \nwill bypass central Illinois without bringing any sensible \nprecipitation impacts. However, the resulting shift to \npersistent low-level southerly return flow on the backside of \nthe departing high will initiate a steady warming trend. Daytime\nhighs are expected to surge well into the 60s for the remainder\nof the workweek.\n\n\nWeekend Frontal Passage & Post-Frontal Cool Down (Saturday\nthrough Monday):\n\nElevated temperatures will hold into Saturday as anomalous \nupper-level ridging briefly reestablishes itself over the \ncentral U.S. This ridge will rapidly break down as a broader \nlongwave trough advances across the western states this weekend.\nMultiple lead shortwaves ejecting ahead of this main trough \nwill drive a surface cold front through the Midwest on Saturday.\n\nAhead of the boundary, low-level moisture transport will \nimprove markedly, pooling surface dewpoints into the mid-50s \nacross the region. This influx of moisture will generate enough \nweak buoyancy to support isolated thunderstorms along the \nfrontal zone. While fast kinematic flow aloft will create a \nclassic high-shear, low-CAPE environment over central Illinois, \nweak instability will keep the overall severe weather threat low\n(less than 15% chance).\n\nIn the wake of Saturday's cold front, strong cold air advection\nwill cause temperatures to plummet from Sunday into Monday. A \ndry continental polar (cP) airmass will settle over the Midwest,\nreturning daytime highs to the 40s and dropping overnight lows \nback into the 20s to start the new week.",
